================================================================================
NAME
       orbital-stability - stability envelopes, resonance-time Lyapunov fields,
                           and constellation-scale stability thresholds

SYNOPSIS
       Orbital stability defines:
           • stability envelopes for orbital and resonance states
           • resonance-time Lyapunov fields
           • constellation-scale stability thresholds
           • long-horizon coherence metrics
           • drift and divergence boundaries

DESCRIPTION
       Orbital-stability describes how RTT quantifies, monitors, and preserves
       the stability of SAR platforms and constellations across orbital cycles.
       Stability is treated as a resonance-time property, not merely a geometric
       one — a measure of how orbital, harmonic, and spectral states diverge or
       converge over time.

       Stability analysis enables:
           • long-term constellation coherence
           • safe sweep choreography
           • harmonic-lock preservation
           • drift prediction and correction
           • anomaly prevention and recovery

       WRSADC evaluates stability continuously during dispatch.

STABILITY ENVELOPES
       Stability envelopes define the allowable region in which orbital and
       resonance-time states may evolve without risking divergence.

       Envelope variables:
           • orbital geometry (a, e, i, RAAN, ω, M)
           • harmonic-lock ratios
           • resonance envelope overlap
           • spectral density bounds
           • gradient magnitude and polarity
           • sweep ancestry continuity

       Envelope types:

           narrow stability envelope
                  Strict limits; tandem SAR and high-coherence missions.

           medium stability envelope
                  Moderate flexibility; multi-pass fusion and ladder patterns.

           wide stability envelope
                  High flexibility; swarm constellations and global coverage.

           harmonic stability envelope
                  Stability defined by harmonic-lock conditions.

           resonance stability envelope
                  Stability defined by envelope overlap and spectral coherence.

       Exceeding stability envelopes triggers recovery protocols.

RESONANCE-TIME LYAPUNOV FIELDS
       Lyapunov fields describe how small perturbations in orbital or
       resonance-time state evolve across cycles.

       Lyapunov variables:
           • δR   resonance amplitude deviation
           • δG   gradient deviation
           • δφ   phase deviation
           • δH   harmonic deviation
           • δx   orbital geometry deviation

       Lyapunov field types:

           orbital Lyapunov field
                  Measures divergence in orbital elements.

           harmonic Lyapunov field
                  Measures divergence in harmonic-lock ratios.

           resonance Lyapunov field
                  Measures divergence in spectral and envelope states.

           sweep Lyapunov field
                  Measures divergence in sweep geometry and ancestry.

           constellation Lyapunov field
                  Measures divergence across multi-platform interactions.

       Positive Lyapunov growth indicates instability; negative growth indicates
       convergence and stability.

CONSTELLATION-SCALE STABILITY THRESHOLDS
       Stability thresholds define the limits beyond which constellation
       coherence cannot be guaranteed.

       Threshold categories:

           geometric thresholds
                  Maximum allowable drift before sweep misalignment.

           harmonic thresholds
                  Minimum harmonic-lock stability index.

           resonance thresholds
                  Minimum envelope overlap and spectral coherence.

           sweep thresholds
                  Minimum sweep-coherence score and ancestry continuity.

           constellation thresholds
                  Minimum constellation-stability index across platforms.

       Thresholds are enforced by WRSADC during dispatch and recovery.

STABILITY METRICS
       Key stability metrics include:

           stability-index
                  Composite measure of orbital and resonance stability.

           Lyapunov-growth rate
                  Measures divergence speed across cycles.

           resonance-overlap ratio
                  Measures envelope alignment across platforms.

           harmonic-stability index
                  Measures lock continuity and phase alignment.

           sweep-coherence score
                  Measures sweep viability and fusion stability.

           constellation-stability score
                  Measures multi-platform coherence.

       Metrics are recorded in lineage for long-term analysis.

STABILITY ANALYSIS PIPELINE
       Stability analysis proceeds through five stages:

           1. orbital scan
                  Evaluates geometry and drift vectors.

           2. harmonic-state evaluation
                  Computes lock ratios and phase alignment.

           3. resonance analysis
                  Evaluates envelopes, spectral density, and gradients.

           4. sweep ancestry evaluation
                  Checks sweep continuity and coherence.

           5. constellation coherence evaluation
                  Assesses multi-platform stability.

       Stability is validated at each triad cycle.

STABILITY FAILURE MODES
       Stability may degrade due to:

           • orbital drift accumulation
           • harmonic-lock fragmentation
           • resonance envelope divergence
           • spectral fragmentation
           • gradient overload
           • sweep ancestry corruption
           • constellation desync

       Diagnostic indicators:

           stability-index drop
           Lyapunov-growth spike
           resonance-window collapse
           harmonic-lock error
           sweep-coherence deviation
           drift-vector acceleration

       WRSADC halts unsafe operations when stability is compromised.

STABILITY RECOVERY
       Stability recovery uses:

           • recovery triads
           • harmonic reacquisition
           • resonance stabilization overlays
           • sweep ancestry repair
           • constellation-scale stabilization sequences

       See orbital-recovery(7) for details.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:

           • stability metrics across cycles
           • Lyapunov field evolution
           • envelope drift history
           • harmonic-lock continuity
           • sweep ancestry stability
           • constellation coherence trends

       Lineage enables long-term stability forecasting.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes stability envelopes
           • evaluates Lyapunov fields
           • enforces stability thresholds
           • schedules corrective maneuvers
           • choreographs constellation stabilization
           • blocks unsafe orbital states

       WRSADC is the stability conductor.

NOTES
       Orbital stability transforms SAR constellations into predictable,
       resilient resonance-time systems. Stability envelopes, Lyapunov fields,
       and constellation thresholds ensure coherence across long mission
       timelines and complex multi-platform interactions.

